#somatic_page(
  title: "Phase 1: Foundation & Safety Container",
  body_text: [
    #align(center)[
      _"You cannot heal in the same environment that made you sick."_
      
      — Unknown
    ]
    
    #v(1cm)
    
    You may feel your breath quicken as you read the words "safety container." Perhaps your jaw clenches, or your shoulders rise toward your ears. This is your nervous system saying: *I have never known true safety, so why would I trust it now?*
    
    #v(1cm)
    
    #rect(fill: rgb("#F5F5F0"), stroke: 0.5pt + gray, inset: 15pt, radius: 2pt)[
      Shadow work without a safety container is retraumatization. You cannot dig into your depths if your nervous system is in survival mode. The polyvagal ladder teaches us that healing requires *ventral vagal activation*—a state of felt safety where the social engagement system is online and the body knows it is not under threat.
      
      Building a safety container is not metaphorical. It is a *clinical intervention*. You learn to self-soothe, to co-regulate, to recognize when your window of tolerance has been exceeded and to consciously return to baseline. Without this skill, shadow work becomes self-violence—prodding wounds without the capacity to metabolize what arises.
      
      The foundation phase teaches you three somatic anchors: grounding (connecting to your body and the earth), resourcing (accessing internal and external sources of regulation), and pendulation (moving between activation and settling). These are not "nice to have" skills. They are *prerequisites*. You would not perform surgery without anesthesia. Do not excavate your shadow without these tools.
    ]
    
    #v(1cm)
    
    *In this phase, you will:*
    - Learn to track your nervous system states in real-time
    - Build a personalized toolkit for self-regulation
    - Practice grounding before, during, and after shadow work
    - Establish boundaries with yourself about when to dig and when to rest
  ],
  image_path: none,
  layout_style: "bottom"
)
